From aa8a713568d72aaa0d58dfc6b46903328232f5de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Colliers <mail@thomascolliers.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 15:39:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] media: qcom: camss: vfe-340: Add TFE variant data

The TFE block comes in several variants that share this register layout
but differ in a handful of constants. Move those constants out of the
macros and into per-variant data, so a new variant is a table entry
rather than a copy of the file: the BUS block offset, the packer format
used by the frame based RDI clients, and the number of bus clients
(write masters) the hardware implements.

While here, drop TFE_CLI_NUM. It was the terminator of enum tfe_client
and served as the bound of the bus overflow reporting loop, which
conflates two unrelated quantities: the clients this driver knows how
to program (BAYER and RDI0..2, named by the enum) and the clients the
hardware implements, which is what TFE_BUS_OVERFLOW_STATUS reports a
bit per. On TFE530 both happen to be 10, so the bug is latent, but the
loop bound belongs to the hardware description and not to the driver's
list of names. Keeping the terminator around would leave that trap in
place for the next variant and for the next loop over clients, and the
resulting under-reporting is silent. The bound now comes from the
variant data, and enum tfe_client is left describing only the clients
the driver actually programs.

No functional change intended. tfe530_variant reproduces the previous
constants exactly; the generated code differs only in unsigned rather
than signed extension of the register offsets and in the association
of a constant addition, both of which compute the same addresses.

Assisted-by: pi:claude-opus-5
Suggested-by: Jorijn van der Graaf <jorijnvdgraaf@catcrafts.net>
Signed-off-by: Thomas Colliers <mail@thomascolliers.com>
